## 8. Configure the Gateway

### 8a. Set the Gateway token

On the Pi, export the gateway token (or add to `~/.bashrc` for persistence):

```bash
export OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_TOKEN="0761ef748b8f0d837b871ec908b5d1f840c792040c8b9f36"
```

### 8b. Authorize exec permissions

On the **OpenClaw VM/gateway host**, set the node security to **full** access (not allowlist, not deny):

> ⚠️ **Note:** The correct setting is `full`, not allowlist/deny.
> Also leave the ask mode as **off** (do not change to `on-miss`).

```bash
# On the gateway VM:
openclaw approvals allowlist add --node minipupperv2 "*"
```

---

## 9. Connect the Node

Open **three terminals** on the Pi (e.g., VS Code integrated terminals).

### Terminal 1 — Run the node

```bash
export OPENCLAW_GATEWAY_TOKEN="0761ef748b8f0d837b871ec908b5d1f840c792040c8b9f36"
openclaw node run --host "instance-20260506-083731.tail2df607.ts.net" --port 443 --tls
```

You'll see a **request ID** printed.

On the **OpenClaw VM**, approve it:

```bash
openclaw devices approve <request-id>
```

The terminal should now show `paired · connected`.

### Terminal 2 — Monitor tasks

```bash
watch -n 2 'cat ~/minipupper-app/tasks.json | tail -n $((LINES-2))'
```

### Terminal 3 — Run the operator

```bash
cd ~/minipupper-app && python minipupper_operator.py -k
```

